    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change\n\nThe spec requires validating the E.164 telephone's **national-number length against its country code** \u2014 `+61` needs 9 national digits, `+1` needs 10 \u2014 rejecting with 400 when wrong. The existing `TelephoneNormalizer` only enforced a generic \"8 to 15 digits after the `+`\" rule, so a wrong-for-country length like `+61 123` was only caught incidentally, and a country-correct total-but-wrong-national length would slip through.\n\n**`TelephoneNormalizer.java`** \u2014 added a per-country national-length table and a check:\n- `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` (`LinkedHashMap`, longest code first): `61 \u2192 9`, `1 \u2192 10`.\n- `hasValidNationalLength(digits)`: matches the recognised country-code prefix (longest first) and requires the national portion to be exactly that length; unrecognised country codes fall back to the generic 8\u201315 rule.\n- `toE164` now returns `null` (\u2192 `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2192 400) when the national length is wrong.\n\nThis composes cleanly with the existing default-country path (`+61` assumed, leading `0` dropped), so `uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 9 national digits) stays valid, and both the create pipeline (`BuildOwner`) and duplicate check (`CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique`) get the rule from the single shared normalizer. The lenient `comparisonKey` still never throws.\n\n**`InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 updated the 400 message to explain the per-country requirement.\n\n## Tests\n- `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123`) \u2192 400 \u2713\n- Full `mvnw test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
